# Time Complexity:

    Time Complexity measures how the runtime of an algorithm grows with the input size (n).
    -> It does not measure actual time in seconds.
    -> It measures growth rate (number of operations).

# Need:
    Different machines --> different speed.
    So, we compare logic not machine.


# Types of Time Complexity:

    1. O(1) : Constant (execution time does not depend on input size).

    2. O(n) : Linear Time (Time grows linearly with the input size).

    3. O(n^2) : Quadratic Time (Usually due to nested loops).
            Outer loop runs "n" times and inner loop runs "n" times : n * n = n^2.

    4. O(log N) : Logarithmic Time (Appears when input size reduces by half each step).
            Eg: Binary Search.

    5. O(2^n) : Exponential Time (Bad).
            Used in naive recursion. (eg: fibonacci).

    6. Big-O, Big-Ω, Big-Θ

        i. Big-O : Worst Case
        ii. Big-Ω : Best Case
        iii. Big-Θ : Average Case


# Space Complexity:
    Space complexity measures extra memory used by an algorithm (excluding input).
    It includes: Variables, Arrays, Recursion Stack and Auxiliary Data Structure.

    1. O(1) : Constant Space
    2. O(n) : Linear Space.

# Space Complexity in Recursion:
    Eg: Recursion Fibonacci.

    -> Each recursion call uses stack memory.
    -> Depth of recursion = n.
    So, space complexity = O(n).

    Note: Recursion uses stack memory, which increases space complexity.